object_content"><span id="S6.F3.pic1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Throughput (M/s)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6.3</span>: </span><span id="S6.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cache performance benchmark. The x-axis is the the ratio of the cache size to the size of the data, with the cache size varying from 1% of the data size to 100%. Keys are queried randomly, with the total number of queries being 10<math id="S6.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> the number of keys. The y-axis is the throughput in millions of operations per second.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Load benchmark</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Takeaway.</span> Non-metadata tables operate faster during load, but the performance gap closes at high loads. At high load factors,
metadata reduces the number of probes for operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We include WarpCore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> only as a baseline as it is not fully concurrent as described in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Supporting concurrent upserts ‣ 4 GPU hash table optimizations ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Subsection</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. At 90% load factor, Warpcore is 24% faster for insertions, 2% faster for queries, and 11% faster for deletions than DoubleHT as it does not perform safe synchronization, acquire-release semantics, or atomic key-value pair insertions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> In the load benchmark, we measure the performance of the hash
tables as they fill from empty to 90% load factor. The experiment is run with
the hash tables initialized to hold 100M slots. In each iteration, the hash
table is loaded to a set fill percentage ranging from 5% to 90%, incrementing
in steps of 5%, and performance is measured for both insertions and queries at
that fill percentage. For deletions, we remove 5% of existing keys at a time
until the hash table is empty. We also measure the number of cache line probes
for each operation at different load factors as a representative of the
performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insertions.</span>
Results for insertion are in <a href="#S6.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 6.1 ‣ 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1a</span></a>. For insertions, P2HT is
the fastest until 35% load factor with a peak performance of 2.037 billion
insertions per second. The second-fastest table is IcebergHT with peak
performance of 2.012 billion inserts per second.
At small load factors, P2HT employs a shortcut (see <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Hash table designs ‣ 2 Hash table design space ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Subsection</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>)
optimization for most insertions, leading to a higher throughput as only one
bucket needs to be loaded.
IcebergHT also suffers similar performance degradation as the table must perform
extra loads in the backyard.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Above 35% load factor, DoubleHT has the highest performance, with peak
throughput of 1.76 billion insertions per second.
Above 65% load, CuckooHT has the second highest performance of 1.426 billion
insertions per second. However, the performance rapidly decreases at high
load factors, with CuckooHT only performing 804M insertions per second
at 90% load.
IcebergHT(M), P2HT(M), and DoubleHT(M) have consistent performance for insertions
at all load factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the load phase, the P2HT and IcebergHT tables feature the lowest probe
counts for insertion, while shortcut optimization is possible. Above 60% load
factor, the DoubleHT(M) table has the lowest probe count at 5.12 probes per
insertion, followed closely followed by IcebergHT(M) at 5.24 probes and P2HT(M) at
5.25 probes per insertion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Queries.</span> Results for queries are in <a href="#S6.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 6.1 ‣ 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1b</span></a>. CuckooHT is the
fastest for all loads below 25% due to its low associativity, with a peak
performance of 4.249 billion queries per second. Above 25% load factor,
DoubleHT is the fastest table with peak performance of 3.96 billion queries per
second.
P2HT, the second-fastest table below 25% load, is within 7% of the
performance of CuckooHT with a peak performance of 3.986 billion queries per
second.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">DoubleHT features the second lowest
average probe count for queries during this benchmark behind ChainingHT, and P2HT(M) and
IcebergHT(M) always perform a consistent number of loads during queries - as
long as there is not a metadata collision, the P2HT(M) loads 1.5 metadata buckets
on average along with one 1 cache line inside of a bucket. With 32 key-value
pairs in a bucket, each bucket spans 4 cache lines. Having to check 3 buckets
in the worst case makes this table very performant when the load is high, and
the P2HT(M) table’s query performance of 2.605 billion queries per second at 90%
load factor is the second-fastest of all tables. P2HT(M) is the fastest of
the metadata hash tables with average performance of 2.90 billion queries per
second.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deletions.</span>
Results for deletions are in <a href="#S6.F1.sf3" title="In Figure 6.1 ‣ 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1c</span></a>. CuckooHT is the fastest table
for deletions with a peak performance of 2.17 billion removals per second. This
fast throughput is due to its low associativity and fast worst-case
performance. P2HT and IcebergHT are the second and third-fastest tables for
removal until 40% load factor, at which point DoubleHT is the fastest table
with throughput of 1.77 billion deletions per second.
ChainingHT, DoubleHT(M), IcebergHT(M), and P2HT(M) have consistent performance for
removals, though ChainingHT sees a rise in probe count at higher load factors.
The metadata tables maintain consistent performance of 1.17 billion deletions
per second and do not significantly vary for probe count either.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S6.F4.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><a href="#S6.F4.sf1" title="Figure 6.4a ‣ Figure 6.4 ‣ 6.3 Load benchmark ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4a</span></a></p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
