span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6.4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Insertion and query performance for hash tales when scaling the size of the table from 10 Million to 1 Billion key-value pairs.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Scaling benchmark</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Takeaway.</span> Insertion performance drops as GPU tables scale due to reduced
L2 hit rate. Query performance does not change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> This benchmark measures the performance of insertions and queries
as the tables grow from holding 10M to holding 1B key-value pairs. This
benchmark measures raw throughput and probe counts as the tables are filled to
90% load factor. Additional performance metrics for this benchmark were
captured using the Nsight Compute tool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
The results of this benchmark can be seen
in <a href="#S6.F4" title="In 6.3 Load benchmark ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>. Most hash tables maintain consistent performance when scaling from 10M to 1B
items, with all hash tables except CuckooHT having less than 20% variation in
insertion performance as they scale. Query performance is consistent
across sizes. Due to locking, CuckooHT sees a 25% decrease in query performance
when scaling.
We omit the figure due to space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Even though insertion performance suffers for all tables as the size of the
benchmark increases, the per-operation probe counts do not change for any
table. Memory utilization, occupancy, and the activity rate of individual warps
also do not vary as the size of the tables is increased. The only changing
factor shared among the hash tables was a decrease in L2 cache hits due to the
increased size of the table. For example, in P2HT, memory utilization remains
over 90% for all sizes, but L2 hit rate drops from 36% to 27% when scaling
the table.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.5 </span>Aging benchmark</h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Takeaway.</span> Negative queries dominate during the aging benchmark and their performance slows down when hash tables do not support an early exit. Therefore, metadata tables age most gracefully as they support cheaper negative queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> The aging benchmark measures the performance of hash tables when
they are aged. In the aging benchmark, all hash tables are first filled to 85%
load factor and then items are inserted and removed in a set pattern to measure
performance as the hash tables age. In each iteration, a new slice of data
equal to 1% of the total keys is inserted and oldest 1% of keys are removed.
Queries are split into positive and negative queries, and a 1% slice of known
positive and negative keys (not in the hash table) are queried. The benchmark
is run with each hash table initialized to 100M slots (key-value pairs), with
1000 iterations of insertion, query, and removal in each table for aging. All operations per iteration are performed concurrently in the same kernel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> Performance is recorded per iteration, and the results of this
benchmark are available in <a href="#S6.F2" title="In 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>. P2HT(M) shows the highest
average performance at 1.35 billion operations per second, followed by
IcebergHT(M) at 1.25 billion operations. P2HT, IcebergHT, and CuckooHT show
consistent but lower performance in the benchmark. The performance of DoubleHT and DoubleHT(M) rapidly drops over the first 100 iterations. This performance
drop occurs because the tables become fully saturated, leading to costly
negative queries and insertions as queries cannot exit early. This can be seen in <a href="#S6.T1" title="In 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> where the cost of upserts and negative queries raises drastically for these tables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">IcebergHT(M) and P2HT(M) are the fastest for insertions and are 28% faster than
ChainingHT, the next best table. For positive queries, DoubleHT(M) is the
fastest at 2.25 billion queries per second. CuckooHT is the fastest table for
deletions, with 1.276 billion deletions per second due to the low
associativity. For negative queries, P2HT(M) is the most performant at 2.956
billion queries per second as it has the smallest probe count
(see <a href="#S6.T1" title="In 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>). The metadata tags avoid probing the hash table
buckets, resulting in a lower probe count. The same is true for IcebergHT(M) as
only 3 cache lines must be loaded to identify most negative queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.6 </span>Caching workload</h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Takeaway.</span> Tables rapidly age during caching: as a result metadata designs out-compete their non-metadata variants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
The caching workload models a GPU hash table managing datasets larger than GPU RAM. The hash table resides on the GPU, while a key-value buffer remains on the CPU. Queries first check the GPU hash table; if a key is missing, it is retrieved from the CPU and inserted into the GPU, evicting an entry in FIFO order if necessary. Evicted keys are returned to the CPU.
The benchmark uses a 100M-item dataset with 1B uniform-random queries generated via OpenSSL’s RAND_BYTES function. It runs multiple times per hash table, varying the table size from 1% to 70% of total keys in 5% increments. A ring queue, set to 85% of the hash table size, ensures the table’s maximum load factor never exceeds 85%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
<a href="#S6.F3" title="In 6.2 Concurrency overheads ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ WarpSpeed: A High-Performance Library for Concurrent GPU Hash Tables" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> presents the performance of various hash tables for the caching workload.
Across all cache-to-data ratios, the P2HT(M) table achieves the highest performance due to its high query throughput. Since every item must be queried before use, either negative or positive queries dominate depending on the cache size. As P2HT(M) is the fastest for negative queries and the second-fastest for positive queries, it consistently outperforms other tables. IcebergHT(M) is the second-fastest table, reaching a peak throughput of 95.7M queries per second, while P2HT ranks third with 95.6M operations per second. IcebergHT and DoubleHT perform slightly worse than P2HT, with DoubleHT being slower than IcebergHT below a 35% load factor. Among open addressing tables, DoubleHT is the slowest, peaking at 39M queries per second due to the high prevalence of negative queries in the benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The closed addressing scheme of ChainingHT makes it unsuitable for caching, as it dynamically grows the hash table. For instance, when the cache is initially sized to 10% of the dataset, the chaining table expands to 28% over the benchmark’s execution. To fairly compare its performance, we track this growth and align its results with the final cache-to-data ratio. Despite this adjustment, the chaining table has the second-lowest performance, reaching 53M operations per second at a 71% cache ratio. Finally, as CuckooHT is not stable and cannot perform fused operations, it is unable to run this benchmark.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
